    "body": "Winter brings some of the busiest months of the year for loggers in northern Wisconsin. Frozen ground lets loaded trucks reach deeper into the woods and haul timber miles off-road to the mill. Western Star’s latest video follows one of those trucks.\nThe second episode of the brand’s “On the Job” series, released Nov. 12 , rides along with Nate Tessmer, owner-operator of Tessmer Trucking in Antigo, Wisconsin. His truck is a 49X, which Western Star designed with input from timber, quarry and oilfield workers .\nThe series also sells the vocational lineup that parent Daimler Truck North America is counting on to grow. DTNA held 24% of the heavy vocational market in 2024, the company said in its Capital Markets Day release . It is aiming for 35% by 2030, Transport Topics reported from the event .\nWestern Star built the series around the people who run those trucks. “Behind every demanding vocational job is someone whose skill and experience make that work possible,” Alex Martin-Banzer, Western Star brand manager, said in the release. “We created ‘On the Job’ to give those customers a platform and help people understand what their work really takes.”\nHauling timber on frozen Wisconsin roads\nTessmer’s day runs from the highway onto remote logging roads. He positions the truck for loading, manages its weight and hauls timber to different mills depending on species and destination, according to the release.\nHis six-axle 49X is configured for a gross vehicle weight of up to 98,000 pounds. On the day of filming, he hauled close to 60,000 pounds of timber.\n“You want to get as much as you can on that payload, because that’s how you get paid,” Tessmer said in the release. “You want to be right up to the legal limit.”\nWinter changes the math on Wisconsin’s state highways. Once the ground freezes at least 18 inches deep, the Wisconsin Department of Transportation can declare frozen roads . Log trucks with at least five axles can then run at up to 98,000 pounds gross, up from the normal 80,000.\nNo special permit is needed, but the trucks must be licensed at 80,000 pounds. The higher weights do not apply on county or local roads unless the local agency allows them.\nThe declaration normally runs from December to late February or early March, acco r di n g to WisDOT . Spring thaw restrictions on weaker state roads typically follow from early March into May.\nTessmer’s truck runs an 18-speed manual transmission and is set to switch to a Detroit DT12-V automated manual, according to Western Star’s episode page . Detroit introduced its DT12 vocational transmissions alongside the 49X in 2020 . Those transmissions launched with an Off-Road Mode for terrain like logging roads.\n“This thing has been awesome,” Tessmer said. “I’ve had it for over a year. I’ve got 118,000 miles on it. It’s built to be in the woods.”\nThe 47X gives the X-Series a shorter option\nThe 49X has a smaller cousin. Western Star introduced the 47X in 2021 , and it shares a chassis and electrical architecture with the 49X. It measures 10 inches shorter from bumper to back of cab, which suits jobs like drywall delivery that call for a shorter truck.\nThe series opened with that truck. Episode one featured Dealers Supply Co., a fourth-generation, family-owned roofing distributor in Portland, Oregon. Its 47X boom truck carries a 40-foot body and a truck-mounted crane for deliveries to tight city jobsites.\nThe brand’s on-highway tractor is gone. DTNA retired the Western Star 57X, Transport Topics reported in March . In a statement to the outlet, the company said “the 47X and 49X reception has exceeded expectations.”\nDaimler builds around vocational trucks\nJohn O’Leary, DTNA president and CEO, made the case for the vocational push at Daimler Truck’s 2025 Capital Markets Day. “The combination of the vocational market’s lower cyclicality and the growth of our service business will further enhance our resilience, long-term growth and overall profitability,” he said in the company’s release.\nDTNA also plans to build its largest U.S. truck plant for vocational and on-highway models, with operations targeted for 2029.\nThe 49X is picking up driverless work, too. Kodiak AI delivered two driverless Western Star 49Xs to Atlas Energy Solutions in September. Kodiak says they are the first driverless day cabs in commercial service.\nKodiak plans to deliver 65 Western Stars toward Atlas’ initial order for 100 driverless trucks. Atlas expects its driverless fleet to reach 100 trucks by mid-2027 .\nBut Daimler Truck does not report Western Star or vocational sales separately. Its third-quarter report and Nov. 6 earnings call did not update the 24% share figure.\nFuture episodes will move into more vocational applications across North America, Western Star said.\n“We want our customers to demonstrate the knowledge and experience they bring to the job, the challenges they take on every day and how their Western Star helps them keep moving,” Martin-Banzer said.\nWhy it matters: Vocational market share remains an attractive growth opportunity for OEMs to diversify their on-highway exposure. With more stable vocational demand, higher market share leads to higher orders and more predictable revenue compared to the Class 8 on-highway segment which is more exposed to freight market boom and bust cycles.",
